{-
   A read of a whole owned local in a sink position is a *move* when the local is dead afterwards -
   Design-Test.md §11.1's P3.

   The rule has no syntax of its own, which is the point: `Failure {claim: claim}` used to be refused
   and the reader had to write `exchange(claim, "")` or restructure the binding, and each of those is
   longer than the thing it stands in for. The departure points are exactly the ones
   `eachTransferOperand` walks, so this file has one function per point rather than one per type.

   **What is asserted is the number of teardowns**, because that is the whole of what the rule
   changes. Every case here builds one `Held` and hands it on, so exactly one drop must run for it:
   two is the bug the check that this replaces existed to prevent, and zero is the leak the other
   direction. `dropped` accumulates each object's own `n`, so a missing drop and a doubled one are
   different numbers rather than both being "not two".

   The rejecting half - the same shapes with the name read again below - is
   `resolve/Reject.Alias.yana`, and the two are worth reading together: what separates them is
   liveness and nothing else.

   **There is no moved-from state.** After the move the local is simply not there: nothing observes
   an emptied husk, no teardown runs on one, and nothing has to be written to make it safe to leave -
   which is why none of these functions has a line after the departure.
-}

let &dropped = 0 :: Int

data Held {n: Int}

instance Drop(Held):
    fn drop(->self: Held) -> {}:
        dropped = dropped + self.n

data Pair {left: Held, right: Int}

{-
   A record-literal field, which is §11.1's own example.

   Nothing in this file's bodies reads `dropped`, and that is deliberate rather than terse: a teardown
   runs somewhere between a body's last instruction and its `ret`, so a body that read the counter and
   wrote it back would store a value it computed *before* the drop and lose what the drop added. Every
   assertion here is therefore made from `main`, after the call has returned.
-}
fn intoLiteral() -> {}:
    let &a = Held {n: 1}
    let pair = Pair {left: a, right: 0}

-- A constructor payload, which is the same departure through a different constructor.
fn intoPayload() -> {}:
    let &a = Held {n: 2}
    let held = Just(a)

-- An array element. The literal owns its elements, so the array's teardown is what runs this one.
fn intoArray() -> {}:
    let &a = Held {n: 4}
    let xs = [a]

{-
   An assignment, which is the case that has no `->` to be written on it at all.

   `->` goes on a *binding* and a plain assignment has none, so before P3 the only spellings were
   `swap` and `exchange` - and `exchange` on an object-typed place returns an alias on JavaScript and
   the write that follows destroys the value that was read. A language whose ordinary move is spelled
   with a defective instruction is one bad field away from a silent miscompile, which is the argument
   for this case more than the convenience is.

   Two drops here and not one: `b` holds something when the assignment overwrites it, and releasing
   that is what an assignment has always owed. 8 for `b`'s own object and 16 for `a`'s, run once,
   under `b`'s name.
-}
fn assigned() -> {}:
    let &a = Held {n: 16}
    let &b = Held {n: 8}

    b = a

-- Into a field of something else, where the field's old contents are what the write owes a drop for.
fn intoField() -> {}:
    let &a = Held {n: 64}
    let &pair = Pair {left: Held {n: 32}, right: 0}

    pair.left = a

{-
   Moved down one arm and live down the other, which is what the drop flag exists for.

   The value departs on the `True` path and is still this frame's on the `False` one, so no single
   point in the body is the place its teardown belongs. `128` either way is the assertion: once
   through the constructor's own teardown, once through this frame's.
-}
fn branched(flag: Bool) -> {}:
    let &a = Held {n: 128}

    if flag:
        let pair = Pair {left: a, right: 0}

fn main() -> Int:
    intoLiteral()      -- 1
    intoPayload()      -- 2
    intoArray()        -- 4
    assigned()         -- 8 + 16
    intoField()        -- 32 + 64
    branched(True)     -- 128
    branched(False)    -- 128

    return dropped     -- 383
